# Ridgemere's wharf and its works by the water: the Fish Hall and its cat, the smokehouses, the salt pans and the salt
# store (salt pans in a rain city), the nets and the seals of the Sound, Wickham's Chandlery oilskins, storm nights on
# the piers. (The rest of the city is in ridgemere.scene.)

scene rg_sr_salt_sand
when topic=smalltalk|work here.region=ridgemere
a : Rain in the salt pans again. Half the salt's gone back to brine. | A Fish Hall buyer says our salt's too grey. | Rake the pans the minute the rain stops, or there's no salt at all.
b[laborer] : Salt pans in a rain city. Whoever thought of it never stood in one.
b[merchant] : The smokehouses take every grain we make, grey or white.
b : It always does. | He'd say anything to pay less. | When does it ever stop?
a : Always. | Anything. | Never.

scene rg_sr_pan_wall
when topic=work|lore here.region=ridgemere a.job=laborer|crafter
a : The spring tide broke the pan wall again. | Rebuilt the pan wall twice this month. | Sun on the pans at last. Salt by the week's end.
b[laborer] : Clay and stone and prayer. That's a pan wall.
b : Again? | Twice! | Sun? Here?
a : Again. | Twice. | An hour of it.

scene rg_sr_salt_house
when topic=lore|work here.region=ridgemere
a : Water got into the salt store by the pans. A whole heap gone to brine. | The salt store's full to the rafters. Good year. | Keep the salt dry, my father said. In Ridgemere.
b[laborer] : Lost a heap, lost a month's wages. Somebody's getting the blame.
b[merchant] : Full is good. Full means the smokehouses can't squeeze us. For a week.
b : Bad luck. | Finally. | Wise words.
a : Very bad. | Finally. | Wise and useless.

scene rg_sr_fish_slab
when topic=smalltalk here.region=ridgemere here.place=market|street|work
a : The Fish Hall's cat stole a whole mackerel today. | The fishmongers never chase the Fish Hall cat. They say it's lucky. | Best smoked eel in the city's at the Sump Market, and the cheapest.
b[merchant] : That cat's earned it. Keeps the rats off.
b[child] : I named the cat. Its name's Captain.
b : Lucky cat. | Odd luck. | The Heights can't touch it.
a : Very lucky. | Very odd. | Nobody can.

scene rg_sr_smoked_fish
when topic=smalltalk|work here.region=ridgemere
a : Smoked herring for breakfast, smoked herring for supper. | The smokehouse fires on the wharf have been burning three days. | My coat smells of smoke even after washing.
b[cook] : Smoked herring's a feast if you do it right. Nobody does it right.
b : That's the wharf. | Big catch, then. | Everything does.
a : That's the wharf. | Very big. | Everything.

scene rg_sr_net_twine
when topic=work here.region=ridgemere a.job=fisher|crafter|laborer
a : Mending nets on the pier all morning. Seals tore them. | The new twine from the chandlery rots faster than the old. | My paws are raw from the net needle.
b[fisher] : Tar the twine. Every time. My grandmother's rule.
b : Cursed seals. | Cheap stuff. | Rest them.
a : Cursed. | Very cheap. | Can't.

scene rg_sr_oilskins
when topic=smalltalk here.region=ridgemere
a : Bought new oilskins at Wickham's Chandlery. They leak already. | Oilskins on, oilskins off, oilskins on. All day. | My oilskin's older than me. Still keeps the rain out.
b[merchant] : Linseed every spring. That's your problem.
b : Take them back. | That's Ridgemere. | They don't make them like that now.
a : I will. | It is. | They don't.

scene rg_sr_storm_night
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=ridgemere here.weather=storm|rain
a : Storm's tearing at the piers. | No boats out on the Sound tonight, thank the Bright One. | Hope the Fish Hall roof holds.
b[sailor] : I've ridden out worse. Not in a boat that small, mind.
b[priest] : I'll light a lamp in the Chapel of the Tide, for those still out.
b : Stay in. | Not tonight. | So do I.
a : I will. | Thank the Bright One. | Me too.

scene rg_sr_seal_hunt
when topic=lore here.region=ridgemere
a : The seals are back on the rocks below the docks. | Old folk say seals are the drowned come to visit. | Seals ate half the morning catch out on the Sound.
b[fisher] : Drowned or not, they're thieves.
b[priest] : The drowned rest with the Bright One. The seals are just seals.
b : Hungry ones. | Strange belief. | Thieves, then.
a : Very hungry. | Old belief. | Fat thieves.
